<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#kola-nut">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kola nut</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as kola nuts</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The kola nut is the caffeine-containing seed of the kola tree, native to the West African forest zone. It was chewed as a stimulant, used in social and ceremonial exchange, and widely traded northwards into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savannah">savannah</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahel">Sahel</a>. Kola formed an important trade good in the economies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Ashanti Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin Empire</a> and was a major import into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kola-nut.jpg" alt="Kola nut" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Franz Eugen Köhler, Köhler's Medizinal-Pflanzen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cola_acuminata_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-190.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
